    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "The numerator of a fraction was increased by 40%. By how many percent should you then reduce the denominator to get a fraction 2 times larger than the original?",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "25%"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "30%"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "45%"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "40%"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "50%"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Set up the equation",
        "body": "Write the fraction as $\\frac{a}{b}$. The new numerator is $1.4a$; if the denominator is reduced by $p$, it becomes $(1-p)b$. We need $\\frac{1.4a}{(1-p)b} = 2\\cdot\\frac ab$, so $1 - p = 0.7$ and $p = 30\\%$.\n\n**Answer: (B) 30%**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Try a fraction",
        "body": "Take $\\frac{10}{10} = 1$. The numerator becomes 14. To get $2$, the denominator must be $7$, i.e. reduced from 10 by 3, which is $30\\%$.\n\n**Answer: (B) 30%**"
      }
    ],
