    "grade": "9-10",
    "points": 5,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "On each vertex of a square, one positive integer is placed. For any two numbers joined by an edge of the square, one is a multiple of the other. However, for any two diagonally opposite numbers, neither is a multiple of the other. What is the smallest possible sum of the four numbers?",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "12"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "24"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "30"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "35"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "60"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "D",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Structure of the numbers",
        "body": "Call the diagonal pairs $\\{a, c\\}$ and $\\{b, d\\}$. Each of $b, d$ is a multiple or a divisor of both $a$ and $c$. It cannot be a multiple of one and a divisor of the other (e.g. $a \\mid b \\mid c$ would give $a \\mid c$). If $b$ were a common divisor and $d$ a common multiple, then $b \\mid a \\mid d$, again forbidden. So either both $b, d$ are common multiples of $a, c$, or both are common divisors (and then $a, c$ are common multiples of $b, d$ — the same situation with the pairs swapped).\n\nSo we may assume $b$ and $d$ are multiples of $L = \\operatorname{lcm}(a,c)$ with neither dividing the other; the smallest choice is $b = 2L$, $d = 3L$, giving a sum $a + c + 5L$. Since $a \\nmid c$ and $c \\nmid a$, the smallest option is $a = 2$, $c = 3$, $L = 6$: numbers $2, 12, 3, 18$ with sum $35$. Other choices of $a, c$ (like $4, 6$ or $2, 5$) make $L$ and the sum larger.\n\n**Answer: (D) 35**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
