        "key": "E",
        "text": "23"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "The last two digits are zero",
        "body": "Let the number be $n = \\overline{abcd}$.\n\n- Deleting $d$: $\\overline{abc} \\mid 10\\cdot\\overline{abc} + d$, so $\\overline{abc} \\mid d$; as $\\overline{abc} \\ge 100 > d$, $d = 0$.\n- Deleting $c$: $\\overline{ab0} = 10\\cdot\\overline{ab}$ divides $n = 100\\cdot\\overline{ab} + 10c$, so $\\overline{ab} \\mid c$, hence $c = 0$.\n\nSo $n = 100\\cdot\\overline{ab}$. Deleting $a$ gives $\\overline{b00}$, which must be a 3-digit number, so $b \\ne 0$, and $100b \\mid 100(10a + b)$ means $b \\mid 10a + b$. Deleting $b$ gives $100a$, and $a \\mid 10a + b$.\n\nThese are exactly the conditions \"both digits of $\\overline{ab}$ divide $\\overline{ab}$\": $a \\mid b$, $b = ka$ with $k \\mid 10$. That gives $11, 22, \\ldots, 99$ (9 numbers), $12, 24, 36, 48$ (4) and $15$ (1): **14** numbers, namely $1100, 1200, 1500, 2200, 2400, 3300, 3600, 4400, 4800, 5500, 6600, 7700, 8800, 9900$.\n\n**Answer: (C) 14**"
      }
    ],
